PR-AUC is the primary metric because only {{ best_model.test_base_rate | pct(1) }} of users buy
in a given week (a random model scores PR-AUC = base rate). The champion is selected on
**validation** PR-AUC among the servable sklearn-compatible models, then evaluated once on test.
Scores are Platt-calibrated on the validation snapshot, so they can be read as probabilities.
H2O AutoML ({% if h2o %}{{ h2o | length }} best of its leaderboard shown in `h2o_leaderboard.csv`{% else %}skipped{% endif %})
and Spark MLlib GBT (distributed, trained inside Spark on the Hive table) are benchmarks.
{% if best_model.key == "logreg" %}
The regularised logistic regression matches or beats the tree ensembles here: the behavioural
signal is mostly monotonic and additive in log-scaled counts, which a linear model captures with
less variance. That is a useful finding, not a failure - the simpler model is easier to explain
and to serve.
{% endif %}

